WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.670
<v Speaker 1>Welcome.

2
00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:04.350
<v Speaker 1>We're with the Ismaili dialogue, the master and the disciple.

3
00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.670
<v Speaker 1>And the question that follows once the inner has been opened, does the outer still matter?

4
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.550
<v Speaker 1>Only the union of inner and outer is faith.

5
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.670
<v Speaker 1>Inner alone rots like fruit peeled before ripening.

6
00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:17.070
<v Speaker 1>Outer alone is a body without spirit.

7
00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.030
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, I mean, imagine you are the seeker here.

8
00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:20.470
<v Speaker 2>You've

9
00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:25.750
<v Speaker 2>You've just been guided through the concealed internal architecture of the cosmos.

10
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:32.590
<v Speaker 2>The formulas you've recited your entire life suddenly possess these profound structural dimensions.

11
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.870
<v Speaker 2>The heavens have been open for you.

12
00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.030
<v Speaker 2>You're seeing the vast, intricate mechanisms behind every ritual you've ever practiced.

13
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.830
<v Speaker 2>The cosmology has been completely laid out.

14
00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:48.190
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, and if you're listening to this, you can probably anticipate the immediate, very human reaction.

15
00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.870
<v Speaker 1>Oh, absolutely.

16
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:57.310
<v Speaker 1>The urgent, immediate question that forms in the mind of the disciple is intensely practical.

17
00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:02.590
<v Speaker 1>You might think, well, if you know the spiritual reality of your physical practices, like fasting and prayer, just an empty motion.

18
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:08.430
<v Speaker 2>Like, can you simply discard the physical effort now that you have acquired the hidden knowledge?

19
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.070
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

20
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:15.950
<v Speaker 1>And the scholar, the master of this chapter, he actually anticipates the question before it's even fully articulated.

21
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.350
<v Speaker 2>He reads the temptation on the seeker's face.

22
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:24.270
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, he anchors his response in a specific Quranic phrasing, and this phrasing is crucial.

23
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:29.390
<v Speaker 1>The instruction is not simply about avoiding the physical environment entirely.

24
00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:33.830
<v Speaker 1>The verse states, do not let the life of this physical world deceive you.

25
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.590
<v Speaker 2>Okay, the life of this physical world, not just the physical matter itself.

26
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.150
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

27
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.390
<v Speaker 1>That distinction is absolutely paramount.

28
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:46.910
<v Speaker 1>Life, as the scholar explains to the disciple, has four distinct layered meanings.

29
00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:47.870
<v Speaker 2>Okay, lay them out.

30
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.910
<v Speaker 1>So there is physical life in this inferior world, which inevitably passes away.

31
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.030
<v Speaker 1>your biological existence.

32
00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.590
<v Speaker 1>Then there is spiritual life in the superior world, which lasts eternally.

33
00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.110
<v Speaker 2>And then there's the knowledge of these states.

34
00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:11.870
<v Speaker 2>I mean, you can possess knowledge of the world's outer aspect, which is the lowest form of life, or you can possess knowledge of its inner aspect, which forms the basis for true eternal life.

35
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:19.550
<v Speaker 2>text is demanding that you recognize which life is actually permanent and which life is simply a temporary vessel.

36
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:25.790
<v Speaker 1>Because physical knowledge, knowing how to navigate the temporary hierarchies of this world, is forgotten at death.

37
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:31.790
<v Speaker 1>The soul takes only the spiritual understanding of physical realities into the next realm.

38
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:39.790
<v Speaker 1>On the day of judgment, the text's promise, an individual will say, I wish I had prepared for my life, meaning the eternal one.

39
00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:45.310
<v Speaker 2>So the question is correctly framed by the disciple then, how do you actually carry the inner into the eternal?

40
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:49.470
<v Speaker 1>Right, and the scholar answers by outlining the crises that occur when the two dimensions are severed.

41
00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.390
<v Speaker 1>He presents the specific cases the seeker needs to hold in mind.

42
00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.150
<v Speaker 2>Starting with the inner alone, I assume.

43
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.510
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the first crisis.

44
00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.510
<v Speaker 1>The seeker who knows the inner meanings but drops the outer obligations entirely.

45
00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:04.830
<v Speaker 1>They understand the cosmic significance of the cosmos, but they no longer pray.

46
00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.230
<v Speaker 1>They no longer fast.

47
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.350
<v Speaker 2>Because they believe the physical action is somehow beneath their new intellectual station.

48
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.150
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

49
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.790
<v Speaker 1>And the judgment on this state is severe.

50
00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:17.390
<v Speaker 1>The individual becomes one who has rotted from obedience.

51
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.230
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I mean, I don't buy that yet.

52
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.070
<v Speaker 2>Calling the inner only seeker basic is rhetoric, not argument.

53
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.510
<v Speaker 2>You could easily argue that the peel is entirely unnecessary once you know what the fruit tastes like.

54
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:33.950
<v Speaker 2>The outer layer is just an obstacle to the pure, unmediated experience, you know.

55
00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.110
<v Speaker 1>It's not mere rhetoric.

56
00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:35.630
<v Speaker 1>though.

57
00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.350
<v Speaker 1>It is a structural argument about severing what Allah commanded to be joined.

58
00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:45.470
<v Speaker 1>The inner meaning simply cannot survive without the protective shell of physical action.

59
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.790
<v Speaker 2>Wait, so the physical practice actually guards the spiritual reality?

60
00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:49.790
<v Speaker 1>Yes, exactly.

61
00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.830
<v Speaker 1>It guards it until it is fully formed and secured in the soul.

62
00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:57.550
<v Speaker 1>Without that rigid physical boundary, the spiritual reality spoils.

63
00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.430
<v Speaker 1>It is like fruit peeled before riping it rots.

64
00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.830
<v Speaker 1>The internal substance dissipates because it is no longer held within the structure of practice.

65
00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:08.870
<v Speaker 2>So without the boundary, the meaning just leaks out and is lost.

66
00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:09.470
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

67
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.590
<v Speaker 1>The meaning decays.

68
00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.830
<v Speaker 1>And this fundamental rotting, well, that's what the tradition refers to as Fisk.

69
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.830
<v Speaker 1>You lose the benefit of both dimensions.

70
00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:17.470
<v Speaker 2>Wow.

71
00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:19.310
<v Speaker 2>I mean, yeah, that makes sense.

72
00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.910
<v Speaker 1>Such a person becomes an enemy of the friends of Allah.

73
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:28.110
<v Speaker 1>and they can never attain their company because they broke the fundamental architecture of the religion.

74
00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.910
<v Speaker 1>They assume they were above the structure that was designed to protect their own soul.

75
00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.070
<v Speaker 2>Okay, then consider the opposite crisis, which is perhaps even more common.

76
00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:51.630
<v Speaker 2>The person who upholds all the physical rules perfectly but remains entirely ignorant of the inner meaning, the disciple might expect the scholar to call this case acceptable or at least like a safe middle ground since at least the outer is intact.

77
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.070
<v Speaker 2>The community sees them

78
00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.750
<v Speaker 2>performing the actions perfectly.

79
00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.910
<v Speaker 1>But the scholar declares this the worst station of all.

80
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:05.470
<v Speaker 1>To uphold the outer while remaining entirely ignorant of the inner makes a person one who buries the seed.

81
00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:06.670
<v Speaker 2>I have to pause you there.

82
00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.830
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay of fear as farmer hiding a seed.

83
00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.110
<v Speaker 2>Is etymology dressed as doctrine?

84
00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:16.830
<v Speaker 2>It feels like a linguistic stretch to make a theological point about religious practice.

85
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.710
<v Speaker 1>I get why you'd say that, but it is not a stretch.

86
00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.230
<v Speaker 1>It perfectly describes the mechanical reality of the situation.

87
00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.750
<v Speaker 1>Think about the physical action.

88
00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:29.710
<v Speaker 1>A farmer hides the seed under the soil, completely blocking it from the light.

89
00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:35.030
<v Speaker 1>The person who knows only the outer hides the inner meaning under the visible action.

90
00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:38.670
<v Speaker 1>The outer action becomes a thick, impenetrable barrier.

91
00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:44.590
<v Speaker 1>Because there is no opening for the light of understanding to enter, the seed of meaning cannot grow.

92
00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.390
<v Speaker 1>The individual is a body with no spirit.

93
00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.150
<v Speaker 1>They are numbered among the dead.

94
00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:53.150
<v Speaker 1>They perform the physical motions flawlessly, but nothing ever takes root.

95
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:56.830
<v Speaker 2>So we have two failed states and the mechanics of their failure are clear.

96
00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:04.070
<v Speaker 2>The rotting fruit where the boundary was removed too early and the dead body where the barrier is so thick nothing can penetrate it.

97
00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:04.790
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

98
00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:08.590
<v Speaker 2>So the pivot for the seeker is the third case, which is the actual doctrine.

99
00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:13.070
<v Speaker 2>Keeping both the inner and outer is the only viable path, a person of faith.

100
00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.710
<v Speaker 1>Right, the text recognize this unified individual as the Muman.

101
00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:22.270
<v Speaker 1>This requires the spiritual dimension paired seamlessly with the outer obligations.

102
00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.830
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, that framework of daily physical practice.

103
00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:26.670
<v Speaker 1>What we know is Sharia, yes.

104
00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.030
<v Speaker 1>Only the union of inner and outer is faith.

105
00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:33.390
<v Speaker 1>Inner alone rots like fruit peel before ripening.

106
00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.950
<v Speaker 1>Outer alone is a body without spirit.

107
00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.950
<v Speaker 2>That is the core spine of the doctrine.

108
00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:41.470
<v Speaker 2>This pairing of inner and outer as a single living religion is essential.

109
00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:46.910
<v Speaker 2>And here is where it completely transforms the disciples' relationship to daily existence.

110
00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.830
<v Speaker 2>Imagine you are in that position.

111
00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:53.870
<v Speaker 2>For the person who achieves this union, this physical world becomes fully licit.

112
00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.030
<v Speaker 2>It is repossessed, not refused.

113
00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.110
<v Speaker 1>That is a profound shift.

114
00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:01.310
<v Speaker 1>The physical world is not rejected by the believer.

115
00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:07.710
<v Speaker 2>Right, because they see inside the meaning of the world's good things, daily life becomes legible, eating, working, gathering.

116
00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:09.870
<v Speaker 2>None of these are distractions anymore.

117
00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:13.470
<v Speaker 2>The inner of the inner does not subtract from daily existence.

118
00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.350
<v Speaker 2>It gives the seeker the absolute right to the world's bounties.

119
00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:20.510
<v Speaker 2>They are the most deserving of the physical world because they actually understand what it is.

120
00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:24.270
<v Speaker 1>But hold on, isn't this just replacing 1 literalism with another?

121
00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:26.350
<v Speaker 1>How is this different from a code?

122
00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:33.390
<v Speaker 1>It sounds like you are simply adding a spiritual checklist to a physical checklist and calling it an enlightened station.

123
00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.670
<v Speaker 2>Well, that is exactly what the disciple wonders.

124
00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:39.790
<v Speaker 2>And to answer, we have to turn to the physical toll of this exact attention.

125
00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:44.030
<v Speaker 2>The disciple asks the scholar a very pointed question.

126
00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:53.150
<v Speaker 2>If the true scholars have supposedly repossessed the world, why do they look so pale, so weak, and seemingly reject the physical comforts around them?

127
00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:57.150
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, they aren't physically sick, yet they appear constantly weakened.

128
00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.510
<v Speaker 2>And the scholar weeps before answering that question.

129
00:07:59.510 --> 00:07:59.910
<v Speaker 1>He does.

130
00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.430
<v Speaker 1>It's A profound emotional moment in the text.

131
00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:07.710
<v Speaker 1>He weeps because the question touches on the immense psychological and physical weight of knowing the truth.

132
00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:11.470
<v Speaker 1>He explains that pale faces record nights in vigil.

133
00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:17.790
<v Speaker 1>The true scholars are absolutely certain of the divine promise and threat, so they hasten to act while they have time.

134
00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:20.270
<v Speaker 1>Their bodies grow weak from fasting.

135
00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:21.550
<v Speaker 2>Their complexions pale.

136
00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:22.190
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

137
00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:26.590
<v Speaker 1>But not because they hate their bodies, it's because their attention is entirely consumed.

138
00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.950
<v Speaker 2>They are distracted from luxury by intense reflection.

139
00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:37.230
<v Speaker 1>Right, that deep consuming practice of thinking through what one knows, turning it over until it illuminates everything.

140
00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.590
<v Speaker 1>The tradition calls this focused contemplation al-fikr.

141
00:08:40.910 --> 00:08:44.830
<v Speaker 2>And they are simultaneously engrossed in the sweetness of remembrance of Allah.

142
00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:49.310
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the continuous holding of the divine name in the heart, known as dikr Allah.

143
00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:51.550
<v Speaker 1>The pallor of their faces

144
00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.950
<v Speaker 1>is not a fast against the world.

145
00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:58.510
<v Speaker 1>It is simply a biological side effect of absolute attention to the next.

146
00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:04.590
<v Speaker 2>Okay, hearing it framed through the biological toll of absolute focus completely changes my perspective.

147
00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:07.950
<v Speaker 2>I concede my earlier point about this being a rigid code.

148
00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:11.710
<v Speaker 2>The physical state isn't a rigid checklist or aesthetic punishment.

149
00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.150
<v Speaker 2>It is an honest bodily record of where the heart has spent its hours.

150
00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:15.870
<v Speaker 1>Precisely.

151
00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.790
<v Speaker 1>They do not consider the world forbidden.

152
00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:19.470
<v Speaker 1>They are simply occupied elsewhere.

153
00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.510
<v Speaker 1>The scholar gives one careful line to explain this.

154
00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:29.630
<v Speaker 1>He says they rejected this physical world only because they were distracted from it by their concern for the next, not because they considered it forbidden.

155
00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:38.750
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, the body grows thin, not as an ascetic feat to win spiritual points, but just as the natural consequence of where the mind resides.

156
00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:39.470
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

157
00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:44.990
<v Speaker 1>The world was not refused, the mind was occupied elsewhere, and the body recorded where the mind had been.

158
00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.230
<v Speaker 2>The father of Imams, peace be upon him, outlined

159
00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:54.270
<v Speaker 2>this generations earlier, actually, in the homily of the God-fearing found in the book The Peak of Eloquence.

160
00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.390
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the homily states they pass through the world lightly.

161
00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.230
<v Speaker 1>They are lean of body, but their faces carry the light of standing prayer.

162
00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.710
<v Speaker 2>Their souls are chased, their needs few.

163
00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:08.110
<v Speaker 1>The vases the scholar describes to the disciple are the very faces the homily already named.

164
00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.590
<v Speaker 1>The lean body and the lit face are not asceticism for its own sake.

165
00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:15.070
<v Speaker 1>The body just reflects the mind's location.

166
00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.510
<v Speaker 2>But you know, that realization creates a massive problem for the disciple.

167
00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.310
<v Speaker 2>This brings us to the deepest issue, the origin of creatures.

168
00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:21.790
<v Speaker 1>How so?

169
00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:29.150
<v Speaker 2>Well, if bodies just reflect minds and if we are all created by the same God, why do people differ so drastically in their opinions and their paths?

170
00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:33.630
<v Speaker 1>It is the ultimate question of divine justice.

171
00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:35.630
<v Speaker 1>If God is just,

172
00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:39.870
<v Speaker 1>Why is one person a true scholar and another completely ignorant?

173
00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:40.670
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

174
00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:44.270
<v Speaker 2>And the scholar explains that external physical instruments are perfectly equal.

175
00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:51.430
<v Speaker 2>Bodies vary in language and color as a sign of power, so people may recognize one another, but the underlying equipment is standard.

176
00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.390
<v Speaker 1>Right, no scholar has three eyes.

177
00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.710
<v Speaker 1>No ignorant person has only one.

178
00:10:56.110 --> 00:11:05.470
<v Speaker 2>Because the external design is identical, the inner instruments of will, intellect, and reasoning are also identical in every chest, governed by the pattern of divine justice.

179
00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:11.150
<v Speaker 1>The Creator would not treat his creatures justly in their outer aspect and unjustly in their inner aspect.

180
00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:11.710
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

181
00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:16.790
<v Speaker 2>He has not placed two hearts in the scholar's chest, nor forbidden any means of understanding for the ignorant.

182
00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.310
<v Speaker 2>The instrument of knowledge is the exact same.

183
00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:23.550
<v Speaker 1>So the judgment falls entirely on the use of the equipment, not the equipment itself.

184
00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.590
<v Speaker 2>We turn to the chapter on the heights, verse 179.

185
00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:30.510
<v Speaker 2>which reads, they have hearts but do not understand with them.

186
00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:34.750
<v Speaker 1>It establishes the ultimate argument against those who fail to employ what they were given.

187
00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:39.950
<v Speaker 1>The argument is not that the ignorant lack the instrument and so are somehow not at fault.

188
00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:44.190
<v Speaker 2>No, it is that they possess the perfect instrument and simply refuse to use it.

189
00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:48.510
<v Speaker 1>The difference between the scholar and the masses is not the equipment, it is the use.

190
00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:55.710
<v Speaker 1>Differential response, not differential equipment, is the entire basis on which divine judgment can be considered just.

191
00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:58.190
<v Speaker 2>So how does the divergence actually happen then?

192
00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:01.310
<v Speaker 2>If everyone has the same tools, how do we end up so far apart?

193
00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:04.110
<v Speaker 1>Well, the scholar says it comes from the root being corrupted.

194
00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:06.750
<v Speaker 1>All came from Adam and Adam from dust.

195
00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:10.190
<v Speaker 1>But generations left behind the inherited divine way.

196
00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.750
<v Speaker 2>The foundational inherited pattern.

197
00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:15.310
<v Speaker 1>Yes, what the texts refer to as a sinna.

198
00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:18.670
<v Speaker 1>The scholar explains this divergence using a brilliant image.

199
00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:23.470
<v Speaker 1>He says people are like archers at one target, identical bows, unequal hits.

200
00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:28.510
<v Speaker 2>The bows being the identical inner instruments, the intellect, the will, the capacity to reason.

201
00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:29.230
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

202
00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:31.470
<v Speaker 1>Think about the mechanics of archery.

203
00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:40.110
<v Speaker 1>You can have a perfectly crafted bow, but if you abandon the proper stance, if you leave behind the inherited way, your footing slips.

204
00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:41.470
<v Speaker 2>The root corrupts.

205
00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:42.030
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

206
00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:45.870
<v Speaker 1>People seek fleeting, immediate pleasures over eternal recompense.

207
00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:52.590
<v Speaker 1>They shoot with the identical bow, but their corrupted intention and poor stance cause them to miss the target entirely.

208
00:12:52.990 --> 00:13:01.190
<v Speaker 2>And the prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, delivered the clear message, but the factions splintered because they abandoned that stance.

209
00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:01.710
<v Speaker 1>They did.

210
00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:06.110
<v Speaker 2>And this leads directly to the practical, everyday application of obedience.

211
00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:12.030
<v Speaker 2>The foundation of action requires carrying out all obligations and abstaining from all that is prohibited.

212
00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:15.310
<v Speaker 2>This material purification, the zakat of the body.

213
00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:17.550
<v Speaker 1>Yes, with the tradition term zakat al-Aptan.

214
00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:21.230
<v Speaker 1>It requires staying in gratitude and treating people as you would want them to treat you.

215
00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:24.270
<v Speaker 1>But the scholar addresses the ultimate test of this system.

216
00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:27.230
<v Speaker 1>What happens when pure necessity breaks the outer rule?

217
00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.350
<v Speaker 1>He tells this devastating story of the thirsting woman.

218
00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.350
<v Speaker 2>Imagine the desperation of this scene.

219
00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:35.950
<v Speaker 2>She was wandering, terribly thirsty for three days.

220
00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:39.310
<v Speaker 2>She feared her children were going to die of dehydration.

221
00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:45.070
<v Speaker 2>She finally found a man with water, but he demanded a condition from her in exchange for it.

222
00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:51.230
<v Speaker 1>Facing the death of her children and herself, she complied with the man's condition solely to secure the water.

223
00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.470
<v Speaker 2>And the second caliph heard of this event.

224
00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:59.150
<v Speaker 2>Acting purely on the outer rule, he looked at the broken prohibition and ordered her to be stoned.

225
00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:02.110
<v Speaker 1>He acted exactly like the farmer hiding the seed.

226
00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:07.550
<v Speaker 1>He saw the outer structure of the law and applied it with rigid, unyielding literalism.

227
00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.350
<v Speaker 1>completely blocking out the inner light of the situation.

228
00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.790
<v Speaker 2>But the father of imams intervened and freed her.

229
00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:15.870
<v Speaker 2>He completely reversed the judgment.

230
00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:18.270
<v Speaker 1>He did this by citing the verse of necessity.

231
00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:23.870
<v Speaker 1>Whoever is forced by necessity, neither desiring the forbidden nor willingly disobeying,

232
00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:25.550
<v Speaker 1>There is no sin upon him.

233
00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:29.150
<v Speaker 2>It completely synthesizes the stakes of everything we have discussed.

234
00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:32.470
<v Speaker 2>The literalist sees only the broken rule and arrives at punishment.

235
00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:43.070
<v Speaker 2>But the one who has united the inner and the outer looks at the exact same physical reality, sees the undeniable necessity of preserving life, and applies the divine mercy.

236
00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:49.310
<v Speaker 1>The same instrument of moral reasoning, exercised by one who has seen the inner of the verse, arrives at mercy.

237
00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:53.470
<v Speaker 1>Mercy under necessity is not a loophole or a violation of the law.

238
00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.030
<v Speaker 1>It is the realization of the verse itself.

239
00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.590
<v Speaker 2>Three movements, one unified doctrine.

240
00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:02.590
<v Speaker 2>The disciple learns that discovering the inner meaning does not cancel out daily life.

241
00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:05.470
<v Speaker 2>It makes daily life finally legible as a doctrine.

242
00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:07.710
<v Speaker 1>Only the union of inner and outer is faith.

243
00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:10.910
<v Speaker 1>Inner alone rots like fruit peeled before ripening.

244
00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:13.070
<v Speaker 1>Outer alone is a body without spirit.

245
00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:19.630
<v Speaker 2>Which obligation have you kept without its inner, or which inner without its obligation, and what would the Union ask of you this week?
